<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ===========================================================================
  CardDemo :: authorization-service
  ===========================================================================
  SOURCE OF TRUTH: the eight COBOL programs of the decoupled authorization
        extension. They are REFERENCE-ONLY and are never modified; this file
        cites them by path only.
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/COPAUS0C.cbl   summary list
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/COPAUS1C.cbl   detail view
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/COPAUS2C.cbl   mark fraud
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/COPAUA0C.cbl   queue consumer
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/CBPAUP0C.cbl   expiry purge
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/PAUDBLOD.CBL   segment load
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/PAUDBUNL.CBL   segment unload
          app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq/cbl/DBUNLDGS.CBL   GSAM unload
        Data contracts come from cpy/CIPAUSMY.cpy (summary segment),
        cpy/CIPAUDTY.cpy (detail segment), cpy/CCPAURQY.cpy (the 18-field
        request payload) and cpy/CCPAURLY.cpy (the 6-field reply payload).
        The relational shape comes from ddl/AUTHFRDS.ddl:L28, which declares a
        composite primary key, and ddl/XAUTHFRD.ddl, which declares an index
        ordered CARD_NUM ascending with AUTH_TS descending.

  WHY : Assumptions: two properties of the baseline single this context out
        from its siblings, and between them they account for every dependency
        selected below. Both are properties of the REFERENCE source, so they
        are constraints this module inherits rather than choices it makes.
        (1) It is the only bounded context whose PERSISTED data originates in
            packed decimal. CIPAUSMY.cpy:L19-L30 and CIPAUDTY.cpy:L20-L35
            declare COMP-3 money and a COMP-3 composite key, so the shared
            kernel's packed-decimal codec is load-bearing here rather than a
            convenience. That is why common-lib is a hard dependency and not
            an optional nicety.
        (2) The baseline splits this data across IMS DL/I segments and a Db2
            table joined by two-phase commit. The migration lands all three
            business tables in ONE PostgreSQL schema, so that distributed
            transaction is eliminated outright rather than emulated. That is
            why plain JPA suffices, and why no distributed-transaction
            coordinator appears anywhere in this file.

  Documentation convention: XML has no docstring construct, so the
        documentation obligation is met by this header block plus an adjacent
        WHY comment on every non-obvious element. That XML-comment analogue is
        defined by docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md and by the migration
        plan that mandates it; it is NOT claimed by the project explainability
        rule itself, whose format clause names Javadoc, JSDoc, Python
        docstrings and C# XML comments and is silent on Maven XML. Every
        comment below names one of exactly four categories: Alternatives
        Considered, Refactoring Rationale, Assumptions, Trade-offs. Those are
        the same four the repository already requires of its own test suite
        (tests/README.md:L544-L549), so this file extends an established house
        convention rather than inventing a new one.
  ===========================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <!--
      Assumptions: the aggregator sits exactly one directory up, at
      services/pom.xml, and is the sole owner of the Java 21 toolchain, the
      Spring Boot parent, every dependencyManagement entry this module relies
      on, and the Checkstyle plugin binding. Stating relativePath explicitly
      forces Maven to resolve that parent from the REACTOR on disk. Leaving it
      out would let Maven fall back to resolving carddemo-services from a
      repository, which for a parent that is only ever built locally either
      fails outright or, worse, silently binds this module to a previously
      installed copy whose managed versions differ from the tree being built.
      The second outcome is the dangerous one, because the build still
      succeeds while resolving different library versions than the source tree
      declares.
    -->
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <!--
    Assumptions: groupId and version are deliberately absent from this module.
    Maven inherits both from the parent coordinates above, so com.carddemo and
    1.0.0-SNAPSHOT apply without being restated. Repeating either would create
    a second place where this module's group or release identity could drift
    out of step with the rest of the reactor, and it would diverge from the
    shape the shared-kernel module establishes for a module POM under this
    parent.
  -->
  <artifactId>authorization-service</artifactId>

  <!--
    Assumptions: the container image's runtime stage copies exactly one
    self-contained executable archive and launches it directly, so jar
    packaging is part of that contract rather than an incidental default.
    Declaring it makes the contract with the image build visible at the top of
    this file instead of implied by Maven's default, which a reader would
    otherwise have to know to infer.
  -->
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>CardDemo Authorization Service</name>
  <description>
    Pending-authorization bounded context: authorization summary and detail
    queries, fraud marking, the FIFO authorization request consumer with its
    transactional-outbox reply publisher, expiry purge, and segment load and
    unload maintenance. Migrated from the eight COBOL programs of
    app/app-authorization-ims-db2-mq, whose IMS DL/I segments and Db2 fraud
    table collapse into a single PostgreSQL schema.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
      Assumptions: every dependency in this module is declared WITHOUT a
      version. Each version is supplied by the parent, either from its own
      dependencyManagement block or from one of the bills of materials the
      parent imports for Spring Boot, Spring Cloud AWS, the AWS SDK and
      Testcontainers. Writing a version here would create a second source of
      truth for that artifact and let this module silently diverge from its
      siblings on a shared library. The repository already holds itself to
      exactly this discipline for its Python test dependencies, describing
      exact pinning as "a hard financial-grade requirement, because a floating
      dependency could silently break the byte-deterministic golden
      comparisons" (tests/README.md:L177-L187). The reasoning transfers
      directly: in a service that moves money, an unpinned decimal or JDBC
      library is an unpinned rounding behaviour.
    -->

    <!--
      Assumptions: the shared kernel supplies the packed-decimal codec this
      context cannot function without, the scale-2 HALF_UP money type, the
      codec for the 18-field request and 6-field reply payloads, the group
      claim to authority converter, the keyset page envelope, the error model
      and the exact 26-character timestamp formatter. It is declared without a
      version because the parent pins the reactor version of this sibling
      module, which keeps the shared kernel identical across every module by
      construction rather than by convention.

      Refactoring Rationale: this is the ONLY intra-project dependency this
      module is permitted to declare. The baseline was a single CICS region in
      which any program could open any file, and the migration replaces that
      with schema-per-service bounded contexts. A Maven edge to any other
      CardDemo service module would reintroduce precisely the coupling the
      decomposition exists to remove, and would turn what must be an HTTP or
      queue boundary into a compile-time one. The complementary prohibition on
      importing another context's domain package is enforced as an ArchUnit
      test rather than left to review, so it cannot be quietly reintroduced in
      source either.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the three online programs of this extension are CICS
      pseudo-conversational tasks whose continuity between screen turns lives
      in a passed communication area. The migration replaces that with
      stateless request handling, so this module needs a synchronous HTTP
      layer but deliberately no session store: the summary list, the detail
      view and the fraud-marking action each carry their own selection context
      in the request path, which is also what makes each request
      independently authorizable.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: Boot 4 SPLIT the HTTP clients out of the web starter, and this
      module needs the half that was removed. RestClient is an API in spring-web, so
      service/RestAccountContextClient COMPILES without this entry; what spring-web
      does not carry is RestClientAutoConfiguration, the only thing that publishes
      the RestClient.Builder bean that adapter injects. The autoconfiguration lives
      in org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-restclient, which this starter brings
      along with its own AutoConfiguration.imports registration.
      Refactoring Rationale: the omission was found by starting this module's own
      bootable jar rather than by any test, and it was not a warning. The context
      failed outright with no qualifying bean of type RestClient$Builder for the
      constructor of restAccountContextClient, reached through
      pendingAuthSummaryService and pendingAuthController, so a task built from this
      module could never have turned healthy. No test caught it because the adapter's
      own tests build it through its package private seam and pass a builder in, and
      each of this module's five context loading tests defines its own narrow
      configuration rather than the application, so the missing bean was invisible to
      a green suite. account-service carried the identical omission and records the
      identical remedy on its own copy of this entry; this is the same fix applied to
      the second occurrence.
      Alternatives Considered: declaring the plain spring-boot-restclient artifact,
      and hand-constructing a RestClient inside the adapter. The plain artifact is
      rejected for consistency, every other Boot dependency in this file being a
      starter; hand construction is rejected because it would move transport
      configuration out of the framework's autoconfiguration and duplicate it per
      adapter.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-restclient</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the baseline reaches its data through IMS DL/I
      segment calls against two databases and an SQL cursor against a Db2
      table, coordinated by two-phase commit. Both stores collapse into one
      PostgreSQL schema here, which turns that distributed transaction into a
      single local one and lets one managed transaction boundary cover the
      authorization decision and its outbox row together. Committing the reply
      intent with the data is what closes the baseline's lost-reply window,
      where a crash between the database commit and the queue put loses a
      reply the data says was produced (COPAUA0C.cbl:L335 commits the data,
      COPAUA0C.cbl:L750-L756 puts the reply outside that commit).
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the value domains being validated are fixed by the
      copybooks and are not free choices. CIPAUDTY.cpy:L46-L49 restricts the
      match status to P, D, E or M and CIPAUDTY.cpy:L51-L52 restricts the
      fraud marker to F or R, while every request payload field carries an
      exact declared width (CCPAURQY.cpy:L19-L36). Declarative constraints
      keep those transcribed COBOL edit rules adjacent to the fields they
      govern, instead of dispersing them through service code where a
      transcription gap would be invisible.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: identity no longer comes from a record this service reads.
      The baseline compared a plaintext password held in a VSAM security
      record; the migration takes identity from a signed token, and the shared
      kernel's converter turns the group claim into authorities. That
      distinction is load-bearing here specifically because fraud marking is
      an administrative action: it is gated on an administrator group claim,
      which is signed by the identity provider, rather than on a user-type
      field the client supplies and could therefore assert for itself.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the health endpoint this starter contributes is consumed by
      TWO independent out-of-process checks, so it is not optional
      instrumentation. The load balancer target group probes it to decide
      whether the task is in service, and the container image's own health
      check probes it as well. Omit this dependency and both probes receive a
      404, the container is reported unhealthy, and the task never reaches
      steady state, which presents as a deployment that rolls itself back with
      no application error anywhere in the logs.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this module is a queue consumer because its baseline
      counterpart is one. COPAUA0C.cbl reads authorization requests from the
      IBM MQ queue named AWS.M2.CARDDEMO.PAUTH.REQUEST and puts replies to
      AWS.M2.CARDDEMO.PAUTH.REPLY, and the migration targets that pair with a
      FIFO queue pair so that per-card ordering survives on the source queue:
      MessageGroupId is the card number itself and MessageDeduplicationId the
      transaction identifier itself, stated in those words by sections 0.4.1.8 and
      0.7.6 of the technical specification, so equal cards produce one group by
      construction rather than by agreement on a derivation.
      Refactoring Rationale: this comment described both identities as
      purpose-scoped opaque HMAC tokens that kept the card number out of SQS
      metadata. That derivation is withdrawn, because a group identity orders one
      card only while EVERY producer computes it identically, and a deduplication
      identity suppresses a resend only while the REQUESTER can predict it, and a
      value keyed from this consumer's secret is neither. The exposure the
      literal values accept is registered as
      D-AUTHORIZATION-FIFO-IDENTITY-METADATA in
      docs/architecture/cobol-to-service-traceability.md.
      (Assumptions: no double hyphen appears in this block. XML forbids one inside
      a comment, so a dash pair written as an aside makes the POM non-parseable and
      the module unbuildable; clauses here are separated with commas or full stops
      for that reason rather than as a style preference.)
      Dead-letter transfer is an explicit quarantine boundary rather than a
      claim of uninterrupted order through poison handling. Two baseline disciplines are carried by the
      listener rather than by configuration alone, so that throughput
      characteristics do not shift silently: the 500-message processing limit
      (COPAUA0C.cbl:L40) becomes a bounded long-poll loop, and the five-second
      get-with-wait (COPAUA0C.cbl:L242) becomes an equivalent receive wait.

      Alternatives Considered: the retired org.springframework.cloud
      spring-cloud-aws coordinate was evaluated and rejected. io.awspring.cloud
      is the maintained home for this integration, and it is the group the
      parent imports a bill of materials for, so the retired coordinate would
      additionally arrive unmanaged and would force a hard-coded version into
      this file.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-sqs</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: this module declares the parameter-store starter even though
      NO document in it claims an aws-parameterstore location. Neither profile
      overlay imports one, and
      config/ProfileConfigurationTopologyTest.noDocumentClaimsAParameterStoreLocation
      asserts that closure. The dependency is here to keep the resolver and its
      client PAIRED, not to deliver configuration.
      Refactoring Rationale: the resolver arrives whether or not anything wants
      it. The queue starter above brings spring-cloud-aws-autoconfigure, whose
      META-INF/spring.factories registers ParameterStoreConfigDataLocationResolver
      for the aws-parameterstore prefix across the whole module, so that prefix is
      claimable at all times and the only open question is what a claim does. With
      this client present a claim fails as a legible region or credential error
      naming the provider chain; with it absent the same claim fails as
      NoClassDefFoundError on software/amazon/awssdk/services/ssm/SsmClient from
      inside environment preparation, before logging is configured. The optional:
      marker covers neither, because it excuses a location that is not found
      rather than a resolver that cannot build itself.
      Trade-offs: the accepted cost is one declared dependency this module reads
      nothing through. It is accepted because removing it would leave the resolver
      registered and unaccompanied, which turns the most likely future edit (a
      reader adding a shared value under this module's own subtree) from a legible
      misconfiguration into a class-loading crash. The paired-classpath property
      is asserted by
      config/ProfileConfigurationTopologyTest.theParameterStoreClientIsOnTheClasspath.
      Assumptions: no version element appears here because the parent aggregator
      at services/pom.xml imports io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-dependencies
      as a bill of materials, so this coordinate is managed exactly as the queue
      starter above is and nothing is pinned twice.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-parameter-store</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the listener abstraction alone does not reach everything
      this context needs. The FIFO attributes that carry the ordering and
      idempotency contract, and the message attribute that substitutes for a
      per-message time to live, are set and read through the SDK client types
      directly. Declaring the client explicitly rather than leaning on the
      starter's transitive graph makes that requirement visible and keeps a
      change in the starter's own dependencies from removing it silently.

      Assumptions: the expiry substitution is a real semantic gap handled in
      code, not a configuration detail. COPAUA0C.cbl:L750 sets a message
      expiry of 50 tenths of a second on the reply, and the target queue
      service has no per-message time to live at all, so the reply instead
      carries an expiry instant as a message attribute that the consumer
      honours by dropping and logging a stale message.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>sqs</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the two maintenance extracts have to be readable and
      writable at a location that OUTLIVES the container the task ran in.
      com.carddemo.authorization.task.ExtractStore is that access path, and this
      coordinate puts its client type on the compile classpath. A filesystem path
      alone was the previous arrangement and it could not work: a Fargate volume is
      shared only within one task definition and infra/modules/ecs-service creates
      one per service, so an extract written inside this container was discarded
      when the container exited and an extract read from inside it had never been
      delivered there.

      Alternatives Considered: keeping the export unreachable and documenting the
      limitation instead. Rejected because the export is the transcription of two
      reference programs, cbl/PAUDBUNL.CBL and cbl/DBUNLDGS.CBL, and a documented
      limitation would leave both with no production caller at all, which is the
      defect this dependency exists to remove rather than to describe.

      Alternatives Considered: routing the extracts through batch-service, which
      already holds this client and the dataset bucket. Rejected because the export
      reads authorization.pending_auth_summary and authorization.pending_auth_detail,
      and only this context's database role may read them; moving the capability
      would mean widening that grant across a context boundary to avoid one
      dependency.

      Trade-offs: the export and the load now cost ephemeral task storage equal to
      the extract they stage. That is accepted because the staged file is what makes
      the transfer bounded in memory and the publication atomic, and neither
      property is obtainable from a streaming upload without leaving an incomplete
      multipart upload behind on failure.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>s3</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the coordinate above contributes the client CLASS and no client
      BEAN, so ExtractStore would compile and unit-test while failing at container
      start on an unsatisfied constructor dependency. The evidence is the
      autoconfiguration's own condition: io.awspring.cloud.autoconfigure.s3
      .S3AutoConfiguration is annotated @ConditionalOnClass on BOTH
      software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client and
      io.awspring.cloud.s3.S3OutputStreamProvider, and the second of those ships
      only in io.awspring.cloud:spring-cloud-aws-s3, which only this starter brings.
      batch-service records the same finding against the same pair.

      Alternatives Considered: declaring the client on a configuration class of this
      module. Rejected because the config package charter closes its roster at
      exactly six @Configuration classes and states there is no seventh, and the
      starter needs none: the client it defines reads the same spring.cloud.aws
      region and credential properties the queue and parameter-store starters in
      this module already resolve from the task role.

      Trade-offs: the starter also brings a template, an output-stream provider and
      a protocol resolver this module never references. That unused surface is the
      accepted cost of one client configured from the settings already in force here
      rather than a second, divergent place where they are interpreted.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.awspring.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-aws-starter-s3</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: PostgreSQL is the target for this context because every
      record it owns is already a fixed-field row with a deterministic key.
      CIPAUSMY.cpy and CIPAUDTY.cpy are fixed-width segment layouts,
      ddl/AUTHFRDS.ddl:L28 already declares a composite primary key, and the
      fraud-marking flow writes more than one table in one unit of work, so
      the store has to offer real referential integrity and a real transaction
      rather than eventual convergence. The driver is declared explicitly, and
      not left to arrive behind a starter, because the schema migration below
      runs on a plain JDBC connection during application startup, before any
      JPA entity manager exists to supply one.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: Spring Boot 4 split the single spring-boot-autoconfigure
      artifact into one autoconfiguration module per technology, so
      FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in
      org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-flyway rather than with the framework
      core, and this starter is what contributes it, together with
      spring-boot-jdbc. The engine and the dialect below activate nothing on
      their own. With this starter absent, every spring.flyway.* property is
      inert, V1__authorization.sql never runs, and the service starts and
      reports healthy against a database holding no pending_auth_summary,
      pending_auth_detail or auth_fraud table, so the first consumed
      authorization message fails on a missing relation after the queue has
      already delivered it.

      Trade-offs: declared explicitly rather than left to arrive as a transitive
      of spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, which contributes spring-boot-jdbc but
      not spring-boot-flyway. The cost is one more coordinate in this list; what
      it buys is an activation that cannot disappear when an unrelated starter
      reorganises its own dependencies. Version managed by the parent.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the authorization schema is created and evolved by a
      versioned migration that ships inside this same jar, so the schema and
      the code that reads it are released as one artifact and cannot disagree
      about a column. The alternative of applying schema changes by hand
      leaves no record that a given running image expects a given column to
      exist.

      Assumptions: the coordinate below is the STARTER, and naming it rather
      than flyway-core is what actually activates that migration. Spring Boot 4
      split its autoconfiguration into one module per technology, and
      FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in spring-boot-flyway, which only this
      starter puts on the classpath. flyway-core alone resolves, compiles and
      starts, and then applies nothing: the spring.flyway.* keys bind to
      nothing and the schema history table is never written, so the
      release-as-one-artifact guarantee stated above would be a claim this
      module could not keep.

      Refactoring Rationale: flyway-core is no longer declared in its own
      right. The starter brings it transitively at the version the parent's
      flyway.version property pins, so a direct declaration would add a second
      editable version site while adding nothing to the classpath.
    -->
    <!--
      Assumptions: this companion artifact is MANDATORY and is not
      interchangeable with the starter above. Flyway 10 moved
      database-specific support out of core into per-database modules and the
      starter pulls no database module, so the build without this coordinate
      resolves cleanly, compiles cleanly, and then
      fails at RUNTIME when the authorization migration is applied, reporting
      an unsupported database. The failure surfaces at application startup
      rather than during the build, which is exactly why it is called out
      here: nothing in the build output hints that it is missing, so its
      absence costs a debugging cycle rather than a compile error.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the published contract is a real consumer contract, not
      documentation. The browser client's authorization module is written
      against src/main/resources/openapi/authorization-api.yaml, so serving
      that contract from the running service is what allows a drift between
      contract and implementation to be detected against the implementation
      itself rather than against a checked-in copy that nothing verifies.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the baseline's operational visibility was job output and
      the operator console, neither of which has a target analogue. The
      replacement is a scrape endpoint whose series carry the common tags the
      shared kernel's metrics configuration supplies, so this module's series
      are directly comparable with every other context's instead of each
      service inventing its own tag names and making cross-service dashboards
      impossible to write.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the controller layer is exercised as a web slice, which
      needs the test engine, the mocking library, the fluent assertions and
      the server-less request harness this starter aggregates. The
      fraud-marking authorization rule in particular needs the security test
      support that arrives with the same starter, because the assertion that
      matters is the negative one: a caller without the administrator group
      claim must be refused.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Trade-offs: real containers cost test wall time and require a container
      runtime on the build host, whereas an in-memory database would cost
      neither. The trade is accepted because the assertions that matter here
      are ones an in-memory engine cannot make. The repository integration
      tests must prove the composite primary key derived from the COMP-3
      composite key of CIPAUDTY.cpy:L20-L21, every check constraint that
      carries a copybook condition-name value set forward
      (CIPAUDTY.cpy:L46-L52), and the DIRECTION of the fraud index, which
      ddl/XAUTHFRD.ddl declares as CARD_NUM ascending with AUTH_TS descending.
      A dialect-emulating in-memory database accepts all three and verifies
      none of them, so a green suite would prove nothing about the schema that
      actually ships.

      Assumptions: these artifact identifiers are the Testcontainers 2.x
      names. The 2.x line renamed every module to a testcontainers-prefixed
      identifier, so the earlier spellings (postgresql, localstack,
      junit-jupiter) are absent from the bill of materials the parent imports
      and would leave these three dependencies with no managed version at all,
      making this POM unresolvable. They are not to be shortened back.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the queue emulator is what makes the messaging contract
      testable with no cloud account involved. The properties under test are
      the ones the FIFO choice exists to guarantee, namely per-card ordering
      by message group and duplicate suppression by transaction identifier,
      plus the expiry attribute that stands in for the absent per-message time
      to live. The repository already virtualises its cloud surface this way
      for the COBOL suite's dataset staging layer, so this reuses an
      established local pattern rather than introducing a second one a
      maintainer would have to learn.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-localstack</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the boundaries this module must respect were
      conventions in the baseline and become assertions here. Expressing them
      as a test rather than as a review checklist is the entire point, because
      a rule that lives in a test cannot rot and a violation fails the build
      instead of surviving until someone happens to notice it. Three shared
      rules bite in this context in particular. Domain types may not import
      cloud SDK or web types, which keeps the segment layouts free of
      transport concerns. No context may import another context's domain
      package, which is the source-level complement of the
      single-sibling-dependency rule stated above. And no binary floating
      point may appear in the money path, which is load-bearing precisely
      because this context's money arrives as COMP-3 and has to stay exact
      fixed point through every hop.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the three rules described above were said to be
      inherited, and inheritance is not something Maven does with test classes.
      It passes a module's MAIN classes to its consumers, so the rule class
      authored in the shared kernel's test tree was evaluated against that module
      alone and never against com.carddemo.authorization; the engine above sat
      here with nothing to run. That mattered most for the third rule, since this
      context's money arrives as packed decimal and has to stay exact fixed point
      through every hop, and an IEEE-754 binary field would have entered a green
      build. This declaration consumes the shared kernel's test artifact,
      narrowed to the architecture package. The architecture-rules Surefire
      execution in services/pom.xml scans that artifact and selects rules by the
      reserved simple name LayeringRulesTest.

      Assumptions: the rules must be authored once in the shared architecture
      package, so this dependency is delivery rather than duplication. The
      configuration does not itself claim that a rules class is already present.
      Version and scope are managed by the aggregator and are not restated here.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      =====================================================================
      Deliberate non-adoptions. Every entry below is a dependency a reader
      might reasonably expect to find in a module of this shape. Each is
      absent on purpose, and the reason is recorded here so that its absence
      reads as a decision rather than as an oversight to be helpfully
      corrected.
      =====================================================================

      Alternatives Considered: Lombok was evaluated and rejected. Its
      generated accessors have nowhere to carry documentation, and every
      public member in this project is required to have some. Java 21 record
      types with explicit constructors give the same brevity while leaving
      each member a place to state its purpose, which matters most on exactly
      the types this context is full of: a decoded COMP-3 amount and a
      five-slot account status array both need somewhere to say what they
      mean.

      Alternatives Considered: MapStruct was evaluated and rejected for two
      independent reasons. Its most recent published release is a beta, and,
      decisively, the mapping in THIS module is not mechanical. It decodes
      packed decimal
      into exact fixed point; it splits the PA-ACCOUNT-STATUS array declared
      OCCURS 5 TIMES at CIPAUSMY.cpy:L22 into five discrete columns so the
      fixed arity of five is enforced by the schema itself; it corrects the
      PA-MERCHANT-CATAGORY-CODE misspelling of CIPAUDTY.cpy:L36 to a
      correctly spelled column; and it masks the primary account number.
      Each of those four steps is a judgement that needs its rationale
      recorded at the mapping site, and a generated mapper has nowhere to hold
      one.

      Alternatives Considered: two retry and resilience libraries were
      evaluated and neither is added. Neither the resilience4j Spring Boot
      starter, whose published artifact targets the previous Spring Boot
      major, nor the superseded spring-retry
      is needed, because Spring Framework 7 arrives inside the Boot parent
      with retry support in the core container. Two API details are easy to
      get wrong and are recorded here because getting them wrong looks like a
      library gap rather than a usage error: the annotation attribute is
      maxRetries and not maxAttempts, so total attempts are one plus that
      value with a default of three, and the enabling annotation is
      EnableResilientMethods and not EnableRetry. The durable retry tier is
      deliberately not in-process at all. It is queue redelivery backed by a
      dead-letter queue at a receive count of five, which survives a task
      restart in a way that no in-process policy can. Recorded in
      docs/adr/ADR-002-compute-platform.md.

      Alternatives Considered: a circuit breaker was evaluated and rejected.
      The only synchronous hops this module makes are inside the private
      network behind
      an internal load balancer, already bounded by explicit connect and read
      timeouts. A breaker would contribute its own open state, and therefore
      its own way to refuse traffic that would otherwise have succeeded,
      without removing any failure mode those timeouts do not already bound.

      Alternatives Considered: a cache tier and a streaming platform were both
      evaluated and both rejected, so neither appears here. The
      baseline has no cache tier, so adding one would change the observable
      freshness of a pending-authorization read for no parity benefit; and the
      messaging requirement here is request and reply, which a queue with an
      explicit reply destination expresses directly, whereas a durable event
      log would require correlating each reply back out of a second topic
      without improving any guarantee actually needed.

      Alternatives Considered: a distributed-transaction coordinator was
      evaluated and rejected, and this one must never be reintroduced. The
      baseline needed two-phase
      commit only because its data was split between IMS DL/I and Db2.
      Landing all three business tables in one PostgreSQL schema removes the
      split, so identical atomicity is obtained from one local transaction.
      Bringing a coordinator back would restore a failure mode that no longer
      has a cause.
    -->

  </dependencies>

  <build>

    <!--
      WHY : ⚠️ Refactoring Rationale: the runtime stage of this module's Dockerfile used to copy
            target/authorization-service-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar, spelling out a version this file
            inherits from services/pom.xml and does not declare. A version bump in the parent is a
            routine edit that touches nothing here, and it broke this module's image build at its
            LAST instruction, after the whole reactor had already compiled, reporting a missing
            artifact rather than a stale literal. Pinning the final name makes target/
            authorization-service.jar a deterministic path and a contract between this file and
            that Dockerfile, so neither may change it alone. services/card-service/pom.xml and
            services/account-service/pom.xml already did this for the same reason; this is the
            third and last module the finding named.
      WHY : Alternatives Considered: matching the jar with a wildcard in the Dockerfile. Rejected
            because a wildcard keeps working right up until target/ holds two jars (after a
            version change with no intervening clean, say) and then copies the wrong one with no
            error at all, which is worse than the failure being fixed because it ships.
      WHY : Alternatives Considered: passing the version in as a Docker build argument. Rejected
            because it moves the coupling instead of removing it: CI, a local reactor build and an
            ad hoc docker build are three callers that would each have to supply it correctly.
      WHY : Trade-offs: the published artifact in a repository still carries the version, because
            this element renames only the file in target/. Nothing in this repository resolves this
            module as a dependency: it is an application, and the aggregator lists it as a module
            rather than any module declaring it, so the renamed local artifact has no consumer to
            confuse.
    -->
    <finalName>authorization-service</finalName>

    <plugins>

      <!--
        Assumptions: the container image's runtime stage copies one
        self-contained executable archive and launches it directly, with no
        application server and no externally assembled classpath. This plugin
        repackages the plain archive into that form, so the image build
        depends on it having run. It is declared without a version because the
        Boot parent manages the plugin alongside the framework it repackages,
        which keeps the repackager and the runtime it targets from drifting
        apart.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Assumptions: the two test tiers in this module are separated by class
        NAME, and the split has to be declared on both plugins or the tiers
        collide. Tests that need no external process end in Test and run here.
        Tests that require a real database or queue container end in
        RepositoryIT and are deliberately NOT matched by this include, so a
        developer running only the fast tier neither pays container startup nor
        fails for want of a container runtime.

        Trade-offs: a naming convention is a weaker separator than a tag or a
        profile, because a misnamed class lands silently in the wrong tier. It
        is accepted because it needs no annotation on every test class and no
        profile to remember at the command line, and because the distinct
        report directories configured here make the tier a test actually ran
        in visible afterwards rather than a matter of inference.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <includes>
            <include>**/*Test.java</include>
          </includes>
          <reportsDirectory>${project.build.directory}/surefire-reports</reportsDirectory>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Assumptions: the integration tier is bound to the integration-test and
        verify goals so that one verify invocation runs it, and so that a
        container-backed failure is reported by the verify goal after the
        post-integration-test stage has had its chance to stop the container.
        Binding integration-test on its own would let a failing test abort the
        build before that teardown ran, which leaks a container per failing
        run. The include pattern is the exact complement of the fast tier's,
        which is what keeps the two sets disjoint rather than merely different.

        Assumptions: the execution identifier is deliberately "default", which
        is the identifier Maven assigns to an execution declared without one.
        The Spring Boot parent's plugin management ALREADY binds this plugin's
        integration-test and verify goals through such an unnamed execution.
        Maven merges a module execution with an inherited one only when the
        identifiers match, so any other identifier here produces a SECOND
        execution rather than a refinement of the first, and both goals then
        run twice. That is not merely noisy: every container-backed test pays
        its container startup a second time. Matching the inherited identifier
        keeps the binding visible in this file, which is the reason for
        restating it at all, while still resolving to exactly one execution.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <includes>
            <include>**/*RepositoryIT.java</include>
          </includes>
          <reportsDirectory>${project.build.directory}/failsafe-reports</reportsDirectory>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>default</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>integration-test</goal>
              <goal>verify</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Assumptions: the documentation gate is INHERITED and is deliberately
        absent from this plugin list. The Checkstyle plugin is declared once,
        in services/pom.xml, bound there to the validate lifecycle phase
        against config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, and every module picks it up
        automatically. Re-declaring it here would create a competing
        configuration whose effective ruleset depends on merge order rather
        than on one file, and the likeliest outcome of that is a gate which
        appears to run while enforcing something other than the parent's
        ruleset.

        Assumptions: the suppression file is wired from INSIDE that ruleset by
        a suppression filter that resolves it relative to the configuration
        location and is marked non-optional, so a missing suppression file
        fails the build rather than being quietly ignored. Naming the
        suppression file from this POM instead would route around that
        fail-closed behaviour and turn a missing file back into silence.

        Assumptions: the gate is also never to be weakened. That means no
        Checkstyle bypass property on the command line, no disabling element
        inside a plugin configuration, no relaxation of the fail-on-violation
        setting or of the violation severity, no shell short-circuit that
        swallows a non-zero exit status, and no error-tolerating step flag in
        a pipeline definition. Layering rules belong to the ArchUnit test
        declared above and are not to be re-expressed as import-control
        configuration here, because two mechanisms enforcing one rule means
        neither is authoritative.

        Assumptions: on return codes specifically. The COBOL suite aggregates
        a mainframe condition code in which 4 is a documented green state
        (tests/README.md:L412-L423), for reasons that live entirely inside the
        immutable baseline and have no bearing on this module. That rubric
        belongs to the COBOL oracle alone and must never reach a Maven or test
        gate. A gate here is binary: it passes, or it fails.
      -->

    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
